course: dictionary
lesson: 9347
title: Spite and in spite of
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence shows which one is used. Spite is a wish to hurt, annoy or upset someone on purpose. «He broke the toy out of spite» means he did it to make someone else unhappy, not by accident.
explain: To spite someone is a verb: you do something just to annoy or upset them. «She locked the door to spite her brother» shows the action and the reason together. Look for clue words such as out of spite and to spite.
explain: In spite of is different. It means «even though» or «without being stopped by», so it joins two ideas that seem to pull in opposite directions. «We played in spite of the rain» means the rain did not stop us. «Aristotle said courage is doing the right thing in spite of fear» means acting well even while you feel afraid.
fact: Spite means a wish to hurt, annoy or upset someone on purpose.
fact: To spite someone means to do something just to annoy or upset them.
fact: «In spite of» means «even though» or «without being stopped by».
fact: «Despite» means the same as «in spite of», and it joins two ideas that seem opposite.
fact: «Cut off your nose to spite your face» means hurting yourself while trying to hurt someone else.
exercise: «He broke the toy out of spite.» Why did he break it? | By accident | Because it was already broken | *On purpose, to hurt or upset someone | To fix it
exercise: «We played in spite of the rain.» What does in spite of mean here? | *Even though | Because of | Until | Next to
exercise: Which sentence shows someone acting to spite another person? | The baby slept all afternoon | *She locked the door to spite her brother | We walked to the park together | They shared their lunch with a friend
test: They kept swimming ___ the cold water. | Next to | Because of | Out of spite | *In spite of
test: Why did the boy hide his brother's shoes? | To keep them clean | *To annoy his brother, out of spite | To find them faster | Because his brother asked him to
exam: Which is the best meaning of «cut off your nose to spite your face»? | *Hurting yourself while trying to hurt someone else | Cutting paper very neatly | Making a kind gift for a friend | Sneezing a lot in the cold
